// Cs7.grammar — C# 7.0: tuples (T3.6.1).
//
// T0.3 merge semantics: re-declaring a rule name APPENDS alternatives (declaration order across
// files); it does NOT modify the Cs1 alternative. The engine is longest-match-wins and an
// equal-length tie resolves to the FIRST alternative (declaration order, Parser.cs:296-316). So a
// re-declared Cs7 alternative is reachable only when it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the existing ones.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser.cs):
//   * Tuple type — ParseTupleType (7920): "(" (ParseTupleElement,)+ ")" (list.Count < 2 ->
//     ERR_TupleTooFewElements). ParseTupleElement (7954): ParseType() + (IsTrueIdentifier() ?
//     ParseIdentifierToken() : null) — the element NAME comes AFTER the type (`int a` = type int,
//     name a). Entered from ParseUnderlyingType (7989) when the current token is "(".
//   * Tuple literal — ParseCastOrParenExpressionOrTuple (12828): "(" + first expression, then if
//     next is "," (12858) or first is Identifier + ":" (12866) -> ParseTupleExpressionTail (12882):
//     firstArg + ("," + argument)* (list.Count < 2 -> ERR_TupleTooFewElements). Each argument is an
//     expression, optionally with a name colon (Identifier ":" Expression, 12892-12897).
//   * Deconstruction — IsPossibleDeconstructionLeft (12231): "var" + "(" + ScanDesignator (12246) +
//     "="; ScanDesignator = identifier | "(" designator ("," designator)* ")". The deconstruction
//     ASSIGNMENT "(x, y) = t;" is an assignment EXPRESSION (ParseExpressionContinued, 11532) whose
//     LHS is a parenthesized variable designation — an EXPRESSION, not a separate statement.
//   * Item1 access — a regular member access ("." Identifier), handled by the Cs1 MemberAccess
//     postfix (Cs1.grammar:723). No change needed.
//
// CS7 only.

// === Tuple type (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Type; the tuple-type alternative REQUIRES a comma
// (>= 2 elements), so it is mutually exclusive with a parenthesized type (a single type in parens,
// which is not a valid C# type — `(int)` is not a Type). Roslyn ParseTupleType (7920). ===
Type =
    | TupleType = "(" TupleTypeElement ("," TupleTypeElement)+ ")";

// A tuple type element: Type + optional name (Identifier). Roslyn ParseTupleElement (7954): the
// name comes AFTER the type (`int a` = type int, name a). The name is a true identifier
// (!ReservedKeyword). `int a` -> Type=int, name=a; `int` -> Type=int, no name.
TupleTypeElement = Type !ReservedKeyword Identifier?;

// === Tuple literal (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Primary (append, T0.3 merge). The tuple literal
// REQUIRES a comma (>= 2 elements). Roslyn ParseCastOrParenExpressionOrTuple (12828) +
// ParseTupleExpressionTail (12882). ===
//
// Disambiguation with the Cs1 Parens ("(" Expression ")", Cs1.grammar:707):
//   * NAMED elements `(a: 1, b: 2)`: Parens FAILS (`a: 1` is not an Expression — ":" is not a
//     binary operator), so the TupleLiteral is the SOLE match. Clean version-purity (rejects at v6).
//   * UNNAMED elements `(1, 2)`: Parens MATCHES (the inner Expression absorbs the "," via the Comma
//     operator, bp 1, applicable at minPrecedence 0) as a parenthesized COMMA EXPRESSION, at the same
//     length as the TupleLiteral. The FIRST (Cs1 Parens) wins the tie -> parsed as a comma expression,
//     NOT a tuple literal. Consequence: the UNNAMED tuple literal PARSES at v6 (as a comma
//     expression), so its version-purity negative is BLOCKED (progress doc, Deviation D1). The
//     TupleLiteral alternative is dead for unnamed elements but used for named elements.
Primary =
    | TupleLiteral = "(" TupleLiteralElement ("," TupleLiteralElement)+ ")";

// A tuple literal element: an optional name (Identifier ":") + an Expression. Roslyn
// ParseTupleExpressionTail (12892-12897). The Expression is `Expression : Comma` (minPrecedence 1)
// so it does NOT absorb the "," separator (the TDOPP Comma fix, T3.3.2). Named union (the
// meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). Longest-match: `a: 1` -> NamedTupleElement (longer);
// `1` -> PositionalTupleElement (NamedTupleElement fails: 1 is not an Identifier).
TupleLiteralElement =
    | NamedTupleElement      = !ReservedKeyword Identifier ":" Expression : Comma
    | PositionalTupleElement = Expression : Comma;

// === Deconstruction (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 LocalVariableDeclaration (append, T0.3 merge). ===
// The deconstruction DECLARATION `var (x, y) = t;` REQUIRES the "(" after "var" (the
// DeconstructionVariableList), so it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs2 "var" declaration
// (VariableDeclarator = identifier, which fails on "(") and the Cs1 Type-based declaration (var is
// reserved, not a Type). Roslyn IsPossibleDeconstructionLeft (12231) + ScanDesignator (12246).
//
// The deconstruction ASSIGNMENT `(x, y) = t;` is an assignment EXPRESSION (Roslyn
// ParseExpressionContinued, 11532) whose LHS is a parenthesized variable designation. In this
// grammar it is ALREADY parsed at every version via the Cs1 ExpressionStatement (as an Assign whose
// LHS is a parenthesized comma expression) — no new rule is required for it to parse (verified:
// parses at v6). It is NOT a separate statement rule here (progress doc, Deviation D2).
LocalVariableDeclaration =
    | DeconstructionDeclaration = "var" DeconstructionVariableList "=" Expression ";";

// The deconstruction variable list: "(" identifier ("," identifier)* ")". Roslyn ScanDesignator
// (12246) is recursive (nested deconstruction `((x, y), z)`); this grammar models the FLAT list
// required by the task (a documented simplification — nested deconstruction is out of scope, D3).
// Each element is a true identifier.
DeconstructionVariableList = "(" !ReservedKeyword Identifier ("," !ReservedKeyword Identifier)* ")";

// === Pattern matching (C# 7.0). ===
//
// The Cs1 `is` operator is a TDOPP POSTFIX (a binary operator at the Relational level), the named
// alternative `TypeIs = Expression : Relational "is" Type` (Cs1.grammar:662). The engine
// (Parser.BuildTdoppRulesInternal, Parser.cs:123-161) turns every alternative whose FIRST element is
// a self-Ref into a postfix operator; ParseRule (Parser.cs:223-335) applies ALL applicable postfixes
// greedily (ContinueFromPartialPostfix, Parser.cs:337-424) and keeps the LONGEST, with equal-length
// ties resolved to the FIRST postfix (declaration order). So a NEW `is` postfix that extends FURTHER
// than `TypeIs` wins for the longer pattern forms, while `TypeIs` still wins the equal-length tie for
// a bare type (`x is int`), keeping the C# 1.0 type pattern unchanged at every version.
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable/Parser/LanguageParser_Patterns.cs):
//   * ParseTypeOrPatternForIsOperator (21-31): the `is` RHS is a pattern; "Note that the syntax `_`
//     will be parsed as a type." (the discard in `is` becomes an IdentifierName/type).
//   * ParsePrimaryPattern (187-252) + ParsePatternContinued (284-398): discard (UnderscoreToken,
//     202-205), var (`var`+designation → VarPattern, 290-297), declaration (type+designation, 358-362),
//     type (367), constant (fallback expression, 250-251).
//   * Switch: CasePatternSwitchLabel(case, pattern, ParseWhenClause, colon) (LanguageParser.cs:10271-
//     10275); ParseWhenClause (10704-10714) = `when` + expression, BETWEEN the pattern and the `:`.

// === `is` pattern (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression (append, T0.3 merge) to add a NEW postfix
// operator at the Relational level (the same level as TypeIs). For `x is int` the Cs1 TypeIs wins the
// equal-length tie (first declaration) → the C# 1.0 type pattern is unchanged. For `x is int y` /
// `x is 5` / `x is _` the TypeIsPattern is longer (or the sole match) → it wins. Roslyn
// ParseTypeOrPatternForIsOperator (21). ===
Expression =
    | TypeIsPattern = Expression : Relational "is" Pattern;

// A pattern (C# 7.0): discard, declaration (type + name), var, type, or constant (expression).
// Longest-match disambiguates: `int y` → DeclarationPattern (longer than TypePattern `int`); `5` →
// ConstantPattern (sole — a literal is not a Type); `var y` → VarPattern (sole — `var` is reserved,
// not a Type/Expression); `_` → DiscardPattern (first, wins the equal-length tie); `y` (a bare
// identifier) → TypePattern (first, wins the equal-length tie with ConstantPattern). Roslyn
// ParsePrimaryPattern (187) + ParsePatternContinued (284).
Pattern =
    | DiscardPattern     = "_"
    | DeclarationPattern = Type !ReservedKeyword Identifier
    | VarPattern         = "var" !ReservedKeyword Identifier
    | TypePattern        = Type
    | ConstantPattern    = Expression;

// === Pattern switch (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 CaseLabel (append, T0.3 merge). The Cs1
// CaseLabel = "case" Constant ":" (Cs1.grammar:901) handles `case 5:` / `case "a":` at every version.
// The new PatternCaseLabel handles `case int y:` / `case var y:` / `case 5:` and the guard
// `when Expression`. For `case 5:` the Cs1 CaseLabel wins the equal-length tie (first) → unchanged.
// For `case int y:` / `case var y:` / `case int y when ...:` the Cs1 CaseLabel fails (not a Constant)
// → PatternCaseLabel is the sole match. Roslyn CasePatternSwitchLabel (LanguageParser.cs:10271) +
// ParseWhenClause (10704). The guard is BETWEEN the pattern and the `:` (an Optional). ===
CaseLabel =
    | PatternCaseLabel = "case" Pattern ("when" Expression)? ":";

// === Local functions (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Statement (append, T0.3 merge). A local function
// is a method-like declaration INSIDE a block (a statement, not a member). Roslyn
// LocalFunctionStatement (LanguageParser.cs:11024) + TryParseLocalFunctionStatementBody (10921):
// modifiers + return type + name + (type parameters via the greedy TypeName) + parameter list +
// constraints + (block | => expression | ;). The version gate (IDS_FeatureLocalFunctions,
// MessageID.cs:131) is a BINDER check (Binder_Statements.cs:556), not a parse check — the parser
// accepts the form wherever the rule exists (CS7 only, per the task).
//
// Disambiguation with the other Cs1 Statement alternatives (longest-match, no equal-length tie for
// the task's forms — the REQUIRED-new-construct is the "(" ParameterList? ")" after the name, which
// no other statement form has at that position):
//   * vs LocalVariableDeclaration (Type Name =/;): the local function REQUIRES "(" after the name;
//     a local variable declaration has "=" or ";" after the name. Mutually exclusive.
//   * vs ExpressionStatement (Expression ;): a bare return type (void/int/...) is not an expression
//     start (PredefinedMember needs "." after the type; a user-defined type is a single IdentifierName
//     that stops before the 2nd name). Mutually exclusive.
//   * vs LabeledStatement (!ReservedKeyword Identifier : Statement): a return type / modifier is
//     either reserved (void/static/unsafe) or not followed by ":". Mutually exclusive.
//   * static/unsafe are reserved keywords -> no other Statement starts with them. async is not
//     reserved, but no other Statement matches "async <type> <name> ( ... )".
// KNOWN TIE: "void N();" (a ";" body) matches BOTH LocalVariableDeclaration (Cs1) and
// LocalFunctionStatement (MethodBody = ";") at the same length -> the FIRST (Cs1) wins -> parsed as a
// local variable of type void (a binder error). Roslyn parses it as a local function (LocalFunction_
// NoBody, LocalFunctionParsingTests.cs:626). Not tested (a ";"-body local function is not in the task
// list); a binder-level difference (progress doc, Deviation D1).
Statement =
    | LocalFunctionStatement = LocalFunctionModifier* Type TypeName "(" ParameterList? ")" ConstraintClause* MethodBody;

// Local function modifiers (C# 7.0): static, async, unsafe. A local-function-SPECIFIC set (NOT the
// full MethodModifier), matching the task's subset exactly and NOT accepting access/virtual/override/
// etc. modifiers (binder-invalid on local functions; Roslyn IsAdditionalLocalFunctionModifier,
// LanguageParser.cs:10882, parses access modifiers only for a better error message). static/unsafe
// are reserved; async is a contextual keyword (a WordLiteral, whole-word match; not reserved, Cs5).
// Ordering is NOT enforced (a greedy any-order loop; Roslyn AsyncStaticFunctions uses both orders).
// NOTE: static local functions are a C# 8.0 feature in Roslyn (IDS_FeatureStaticLocalFunctions,
// MessageID.cs:183), but the task requires them in CS7 (progress doc, Deviation D2).
LocalFunctionModifier =
    | "static"
    | "async"
    | "unsafe";

// ============================================================================
// T3.6.4 — C# 7.0 ref semantics.
//
// Four CS7 features (ref return / ref readonly return / ref local / out var), plus the `ref`
// EXPRESSION (the `ref _y` in a ref-local initializer and in `return ref _x`):
//   * `ref` return — `ref int M() { return _x; }` (the `ref` is a modifier BEFORE the return type).
//   * `ref readonly` return — `ref readonly int M() { return _x; }` (the `ref readonly` modifiers).
//   * `ref` local — `ref int x = ref _y;` (the `ref` is a modifier BEFORE the local variable's type).
//   * `out var` — `M(out var x)` (an `out` argument with an implicit type `var`).
//   * `ref` expression — `ref _y` (a `ref` prefix operator on an expression).
//
// Roslyn refs (C:\RSDN\roslyn, main, src/Compilers/CSharp/Portable):
//   * `ref` return — ParseReturnType (Parser/LanguageParser.cs:3314) eats a leading `ref` /
//     `ref readonly` before the type. Confirmed by RefReadonlyTests.cs:28 (RefReadonlyReturn_CSharp7,
//     `ref readonly int M<T>()`).
//   * `ref` local — ParseLocalDeclarationStatement (10482) eats a leading `ref` before the type.
//     Confirmed by StatementParsingTests.cs:767 (TestRefLocalDeclarationStatement, `ref T a;`), :793
//     (TestRefLocalDeclarationStatementWithInitializer, `ref T a = ref b;` — the initializer is a
//     RefExpression `ref b`), :824 (multiple initializers).
//   * `out var` — ParseArgumentExpression (12581) handles an `out` argument with a variable
//     declaration (implicit type). Confirmed by DeclarationParsingTests.cs:6580 (ParseOutVar,
//     `M(out var x);`).
//   * `ref` expression — ParsePrimaryExpressionWithoutPostfix (12068-12076): a `ref` token starts a
//     RefExpression(refKeyword, ParseExpressionCore()) — a `ref` + expression.
//   * Version gates: `ref` return / `ref` local / `out var` / `ref` expression are C# 7.0 (the task's
//     scope). `ref readonly` is C# 7.2 in Roslyn (RefReadonlyTests.cs:48, ERR_FeatureNotAvailable-
//     InVersion7_1 "readonly references ... 7.2") — the task requires it in CS7 (Deviation D1).
//
// CS7 only.

// === `ref` / `readonly` method modifiers (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 MethodModifier; APPENDS "ref" and
// "readonly". The Cs1 Method (Cs1.grammar:265) is `MethodModifier* Type TypeName "(" ...`, so a leading
// `ref` / `ref readonly` is consumed by the merged `MethodModifier*` and the return type follows.
// `ref`/`readonly` are reserved keywords (Cs1 ReservedKeyword:591/590), so neither can be a method NAME
// or a Type; and `readonly` is a FIELD modifier elsewhere (FieldModifier, Cs1.grammar:162 — a DIFFERENT
// rule), so there is no conflict with `readonly` fields. Roslyn ParseReturnType (3314) +
// RefReadonlyTests.cs:28. Named union (the meta-grammar forbids `|` inside a group). ===
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * `ref int M() { }` (ref return): Field FAILS (`ref` is not a FieldModifier; Type=ref fails — ref is
//     reserved); Method -> MethodModifier=ref, Type=int, TypeName=M, (), MethodBody. Sole match.
//   * `ref readonly int M() { }`: Method -> MethodModifier=ref, readonly, Type=int, ... Sole match.
//   * `readonly int x;` (a readonly FIELD, every version): Field -> FieldModifier=readonly, Type=int,
//     VariableDeclarator=x, ; (matches). Method -> MethodModifier=readonly, Type=int, TypeName=x, then
//     `(` expected but `;` found -> FAILS. So Field is the sole match (no regression at any version).
//
// Version purity: at v6 `ref`/`readonly` are not MethodModifiers, so `ref int M() { }` / `ref readonly
// int M() { }` REJECT (MethodModifier* matches zero; Type=ref fails — ref is reserved). At v7 they PARSE.
MethodModifier =
    | "ref"
    | "readonly";

// === `ref` local (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 LocalVariableDeclaration (append, T0.3 merge). The Cs7
// alternative REQUIRES the "ref" literal (REQUIRED-new-construct), so it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the
// Cs1 Type-based alternative (ref is reserved, not a Type), the Cs2 "var" alternative (starts with "var"),
// and the Cs7 DeconstructionDeclaration (starts with "var"). The initializer `ref _y` is a RefExpression
// (the `ref` expression rule below). Roslyn ParseLocalDeclarationStatement (10482) +
// StatementParsingTests.cs:767/793/824. ===
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * `ref int x = ref _y;` (ref local): Cs1 LocalVariableDeclaration FAILS (Type=ref — ref is reserved);
//     Cs2/Cs7 "var" alternatives FAIL (start with "var"); ExpressionStatement FAILS (`ref int` is not an
//     Expression — int is not an expression start, so the RefExpr operand fails); RefLocalDeclaration ->
//     "ref", Type=int, VariableDeclarator=(x = ref _y), ;. Sole match.
//
// Version purity: at v6 "ref" is not a local-declaration modifier, so `ref int x = ref _y;` REJECT (no
// LocalVariableDeclaration alternative matches; ExpressionStatement fails). At v7 it PARSES.
LocalVariableDeclaration =
    | RefLocalDeclaration = "ref" Type VariableDeclarator ("," VariableDeclarator)* ";";

// === `out var` argument (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs4 Argument (append, T0.3 merge). The Cs7 alternative
// REQUIRES the "out" "var" prefix (REQUIRED-new-construct), so it is MUTUALLY EXCLUSIVE with the Cs4
// NamedArgument (needs an Identifier ":" — "out" is reserved, not an Identifier) and PositionalArgument
// (needs an Expression — "out" is reserved, not an expression start). The value is a simple name (a true
// identifier, not reserved). Roslyn ParseArgumentExpression (12581) + DeclarationParsingTests.cs:6580
// (ParseOutVar, `M(out var x);`). ===
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, no equal-length tie):
//   * `out var x` (out var): NamedArgument FAILS (out is reserved, not an Identifier); PositionalArgument
//     FAILS (out is reserved, not an expression start); OutVarArgument -> "out", "var", Identifier=x.
//     Sole match.
//   * `out x` (a plain out argument): OutVarArgument FAILS (x is not "var"); NamedArgument/Positional-
//     Argument FAIL (out is reserved). NO alternative matches -> `M(out x)` REJECTS (a PRE-EXISTING GAP —
//     the plain `out`/`ref` argument forms are not modeled; Deviation D2).
//
// Version purity: at v6 the "out var" alternative is absent, so `M(out var x)` REJECT (both Cs4 Argument
// alternatives fail on the reserved "out"). At v7 it PARSES.
Argument =
    | OutVarArgument = "out" "var" !ReservedKeyword Identifier;

// === `ref` expression (C# 7.0). Re-declares Cs1 Expression (append, T0.3 merge); APPENDS the RefExpr
// prefix. A `ref` prefix operator (like the Cs5 await, Cs5.grammar:74): `ref` + expression. The operand
// is `Expression : Unary` (a ReqRef with Precedence = the Unary level, RuleGenerator.cs:90-94):
// minPrecedence = Unary's binding power, so the operand binds tighter than all binary operators
// (Multiplicative and below) — `ref x + y` = `(ref x) + y` — and the Comma (binding power 1, the lowest)
// is NOT applicable, so the operand does NOT absorb a following `,` in a comma-separated context. Roslyn
// ParsePrimaryExpressionWithoutPostfix (LanguageParser.cs:12068-12076): a `ref` token starts a
// RefExpression(refKeyword, ParseExpressionCore()). ===
//
// Disambiguation (longest-match, no equal-length tie): `ref` is a reserved keyword (Cs1 ReservedKeyword),
// so it is NOT an IdentifierName and NO other Expression alternative starts with it. `ref _y` -> RefExpr
// (sole match). A bare `ref` (no operand) -> RefExpr fails (no expression after `ref`); no other
// alternative matches -> the parse fails (correct: a bare `ref` is not an expression).
//
// Version purity: at v6 the RefExpr alternative is absent, so `ref _y` REJECT (no Expression alternative
// matches the reserved "ref"). At v7 it PARSES.
Expression =
    | RefExpr = "ref" Expression : Unary;
